/-!
# The common spine

One small family of reusable theorems, and the instances that earn the right to
quote them.

* `Spine.AnchoredGram` — anchored exterior coercivity in arbitrary rank:
  `det Gram(f + t₀, t₁, …, t_r) ≥ ‖f‖² det Gram(t₁, …, t_r)`, with the exact
  identity behind it and the strict nondegeneracy gate.
* `Spine.TransverseGram` — the domain-neutral transverse Gram core owned once:
  nonnegativity of `area2`, the projection identity
  `‖‖a‖²b − ⟪a,b⟫a‖² = ‖a‖² area2 a b`, vanishing exactly on collinear pairs,
  Lagrange's identity as a sum of two-by-two minors, and the
  coercive-or-nearly-aligned dichotomy.
* `Spine.FiniteReindex` — finite reindexing owned once: permutation invariance
  of a weighted fold, the promotion of two duplicate-free enumerations with the
  same membership to an actual permutation, the fibre/deletion decomposition,
  and the layers above it (mean, moments, Gram entries, the anchored area and
  the Gram determinant).  `Integration.EnumInvariance` consumes it.
* `Spine.SchurComplement` — hidden-variable elimination at the level of
  quadratic forms: the Schur complement is the minimum of the coupled form over
  the hidden direction, it inherits a `δ − η` coercivity floor, and eliminating
  a direction `u` from `‖x • u + y‖²` returns exactly `area2 u y / ‖u‖²`.
* `Spine.SchurBlock` — the same elimination for a *vector* hidden block,
  `S = D − Bᵀ A⁻¹ B`: completing the square in the block, the minimum
  characterisation (`schurB_isLeast`), the `S ≥ (δ − η)` coercivity transfer,
  and `schurB_eq_schur` showing the scalar file is the rank-one case.
* `Spine.MeanSplit` — the fixed ⊕ mean-zero splitting of
  `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)`, and the coercivity theorem transported to it.
* `Spine.TernaryPhase` — the exact `C₃` phase algebra, character orthogonality,
  the realification `ℝ[C₃] = ℝ_triv ⊕ ℝ²_rot` (no invariant line in the
  transverse plane), and the binary contrast that makes the ternary case the
  first precise one.
* `Spine.TraceNorm` — trace/norm descent, with the proved caveat that the
  maximal real subfield of `ℚ(ζ₃)` is `ℚ`.
* `Spine.FixedLocus` — the involution split, with complex conjugation and the
  Riemann critical line as fixed loci.
* `Spine.Observer` — the observer interface: a domain, a map into the structured
  carrier, and the nondegeneracy witnesses the inherited theorem needs; the
  Monster five-plane and cuisine taste instances; the degeneracy control.
* `Spine.TritCarrier` — the corpus's own balanced-ternary digit carrier wired
  into the spine by explicit equivariance: `rotTrit` is intertwined with the
  cyclic shift, digit negation with a linear reflection, and the two satisfy the
  dihedral relation `ρσ = σ²ρ`.  The reflection has a real eigenvector in the
  transverse plane and the rotation provably has none, which is the structural
  cause of the corpus's digit-level `inv ≠ rot` obstruction.
* `Spine.Duality` — which operator a carrier actually admits.  Negatively: every
  alternating form on an odd-dimensional real space is degenerate, so the
  five-dimensional taste carrier has no symplectic structure and therefore no
  Hamiltonian.  Positively: the Legendre–Fenchel conjugate, Fenchel–Young, and
  the marginal-rate theorem identifying the dual coordinate with the derivative
  of the acceptability functional at an optimum — with the gate that a flat
  functional has no finite conjugate in any direction.
* `Spine.OggGrid` — the `{5,17,29} + {0,42}` affine grid inside the Ogg set, and
  three negative controls (translation ≠ pointed-sign map; the mod-9 fibres are
  not five blocks of three; no free `C₃` action preserves the `ℚ(√−7)` splitting
  class, so the internal `5 × 3` carrier does not transport).
* `Spine.EqualCardinality` — equal cardinality is not a map.
* `Spine.TwoRegime` — the two-regime Fourier envelope `min (‖f‖₁, ‖f'‖₁/(2π|ξ|))`
  and a summation theorem that uses local counts only, so no minimum-separation
  hypothesis is required.
* `Spine.RiemannBridge` — the narrow, hypothesis-explicit bridge from the
  two-regime envelope and the anchored Gram surplus to the RH analytic lane of
  `Agda/DASHI/Analysis/Riemann*`: the cosh taper coercivity and plateau second
  moment (the two gaps that lane marks unformalised), the real content of the
  grid and Poisson retention ledgers, the aggregate kernel envelope under an
  explicit kernel-identification hypothesis, and two honest negatives — the
  abstract producer socket admits a contentless inhabitant, and the envelope
  alone does not dominate an arithmetic grid tail.  Nothing there asserts the
  Riemann hypothesis or any statement about zeta zeros.
* `Spine.EndToEnd` — all of the above as one checked record.
-/
